WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.970
A young lava flow can overlie an older flow, establishing their

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:09.630
order. Weathering and surface development add clues, while suitable isotope systems

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.610
or buried charcoal can constrain eruption ages.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.230
Hazard evaluation needs the sequence and timing of activity.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:20.090
A dated past eruption alone does not schedule the next one.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.970
A fault is younger than deposits it offsets and older than

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.310
unaffected cover that seals its trace.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.590
Scarps also degrade through erosion.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:33.090
Along rivers, floodplains and raised terraces preserve different flooding histories.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:37.770
Soil, charcoal and surface ages can extend evidence beyond short instrumental

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.930
records, with the meaning of each dated sample kept explicit.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.650
Mineral exploration asks when an intrusion released fluids and how mineralized

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.590
fractures relate to later rocks.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.330
Oil and gas exploration also needs event order:

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:54.530
reservoir, folding, migration, trapping and possible later escape.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.710
The source anticline illustrates why a favorable rock sequence is only

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.030
part of a resource history.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:05.810
Sedimentary basins can store groundwater in connected pore spaces, while granite

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.330
may carry water mainly through fractures.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:12.810
Fossils, relative relationships and dated volcanic beds help reconstruct the basin.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.670
Isotopes in groundwater can address when that water entered the system,

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.850
a different question from when the enclosing rock formed.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:24.810
An older wall fragment reused in younger masonry illustrates inclusions.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:29.770
Successive occupations can follow superposition, while tree rings and carbon-fourteen constrain

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:34.370
suitable recent material. Much older human-ancestor sites may require dated volcanic

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:37.650
layers and fossil correlation. Choose the method for the material and

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:40.070
timescale, and preserve its position within the site.
